WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.867
TensorRT.

2
00:00:02.167 --> 00:00:06.757
What if you could make inference several times faster without changing the model?

3
00:00:06.757 --> 00:00:08.400
That is what a compiler does.

4
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.500
TensorRT is NVIDIA's inference compiler.

5
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:15.567
It takes a trained model, usually through ONNX.

6
00:00:15.867 --> 00:00:19.933
And it builds a highly optimized engine for one specific GPU.

7
00:00:21.133 --> 00:00:23.567
Three optimizations do the heavy lifting.

8
00:00:23.867 --> 00:00:30.933
Operator fusion merges steps like convolution, bias, and ReLU into one kernel, skipping memory round trips.

9
00:00:31.233 --> 00:00:36.533
Kernel auto tuning benchmarks candidates on your actual GPU and keeps the fastest.

10
00:00:36.833 --> 00:00:44.600
Precision calibration drops weights to FP sixteen or INT eight, using a calibration set to protect accuracy.

11
00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:47.067
Here is the pipeline.

12
00:00:47.367 --> 00:00:48.833
Start with the ONNX model.

13
00:00:49.133 --> 00:00:51.800
The compiler fuses, tunes, and calibrates.

14
00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:55.433
The result is an engine file for that exact GPU.

15
00:00:56.633 --> 00:00:59.600
Now the payoff, measured on the exoplanet CNN.

16
00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:02.467
PyTorch eager takes twenty three milliseconds.

17
00:01:02.767 --> 00:01:04.867
The FP sixteen engine takes four.

18
00:01:05.167 --> 00:01:11.367
And INT eight takes two and a half milliseconds, with about zero point two percent accuracy loss.

19
00:01:12.567 --> 00:01:13.933
Here are the two commands.

20
00:01:14.233 --> 00:01:18.100
The first compiles the ONNX file into an engine, in half precision.

21
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.767
We fix the input shape the engine will serve.

22
00:01:21.067 --> 00:01:25.867
The second command times a thousand runs and reports latency percentiles.

23
00:01:27.067 --> 00:01:28.400
There is one big trade.

24
00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:35.713
Engines are compiled per GPU model and input shape, so an A100 engine will not run on a T4.

25
00:01:35.713 --> 00:01:38.333
Build it in CI on the serving GPU.

26
00:01:39.533 --> 00:01:40.545
Let's recap.

27
00:01:40.545 --> 00:01:44.000
TensorRT is a compiler for one exact GPU.

28
00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:47.600
Fusion, tuning, and lower precision deliver the speed.

29
00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:50.867
Our example went from twenty three milliseconds to four.

30
00:01:51.167 --> 00:01:57.381
Remember the chain: ONNX, then Triton for plumbing, then TensorRT for per GPU speed.

31
00:01:57.381 --> 00:01:59.600
Next up: speculative decoding.
